WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.627 --> 00:00:06.135
OLIVIA: Hello! And welcome to People in the News . This week Ben will be reporting on

2
00:00:06.136 --> 00:00:10.359
Aung San Suu Kyi. She's the Nobel Peace Prize winner who has dedicated her life to

3
00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:18.287
the nonviolent struggle for freedom and democracy in Myanmar. Over to you, Ben. BEN: Hi, everyone! Aung

4
00:00:18.288 --> 00:00:20.204
San Suu Kyi, referred to as "The Lady" by her supporters, was born on June 19,

5
00:00:20.205 --> 00:00:29.696
1945. Her father—General Aung San—is considered a national hero in Myanmar because he helped this small Southeast

6
00:00:29.697 --> 00:00:39.946
Asian country win its independence from the British. Her mother, Khin Kyi, was an important diplomat. Unfortunately,

7
00:00:39.947 --> 00:00:43.673
when Aung San Suu Kyi was only two years old, her father was assassinated. She was

8
00:00:43.674 --> 00:00:50.520
raised by her mother and eventually moved to England to study at the University of Oxford. While

9
00:00:50.521 --> 00:00:56.266
there she met her future husband, Michael Aris. They had two children and lived mostly in

10
00:00:56.267 --> 00:01:00.088
England until 1988, when Aung San Suu Kyi returned to Myanmar to take care of her mother,

11
00:01:00.089 --> 00:01:09.493
who had become ill. At that time thousands of people were rioting in the streets of Myanmar.

12
00:01:09.494 --> 00:01:13.918
They were protesting the country's military rulers, whose government was very corrupt and had made the

13
00:01:13.919 --> 00:01:21.243
country very poor. But the rulers refused to give up control and ordered many of the protesters

14
00:01:21.244 --> 00:01:26.575
slaughtered. Although she was quiet and reserved, Aung San Suu Kyi was from a political family,

15
00:01:26.576 --> 00:01:35.965
and she believed it was her duty to get involved in the struggle for freedom. So less

16
00:01:35.966 --> 00:01:41.138
than a month after her arrival in Myanmar, she gave her first big speech. An enormous crowd—somewhere

17
00:01:41.139 --> 00:01:49.623
between 300,000 and 1,000,000 people—gathered at the Shwedagon Pagoda to hear what she had to say.

18
00:01:49.624 --> 00:01:55.724
Her message was that the military government should step down and allow free elections to take place.

19
00:01:55.725 --> 00:02:01.532
She also declared that the people of Myanmar needed to be unified and never resort to

20
00:02:01.533 --> 00:02:07.039
violence. Soon Aung San Suu Kyi was traveling around the country, organizing protests. The government wanted to

21
00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:12.609
silence her, and in 1989 she was placed under house arrest. That meant she had to stay

22
00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:17.518
in her house—she couldn't even take a walk down the street. Aung San Suu Kyi was

23
00:02:17.519 --> 00:02:21.514
a prisoner in her own home. The following year Myanmar held elections for a new legislature. Even

24
00:02:21.515 --> 00:02:25.947
though Aung San Suu Kyi couldn't participate in the elections, her political party, the National League

25
00:02:25.948 --> 00:02:35.091
for Democracy, won a majority of the seats. OLIVIA: So what happened? BEN: The military government ignored

26
00:02:35.092 --> 00:02:37.341
these results and didn't allow the legislature to meet. Countries all around the world denounced Myanmar's rulers

27
00:02:37.342 --> 00:02:42.742
and called for Aung San Suu Kyi to be released. In 1991 she was awarded the

28
00:02:42.743 --> 00:02:49.417
Nobel Peace Prize for her commitment to democracy and nonviolence. After six years of being imprisoned in

29
00:02:49.418 --> 00:02:56.145
her home, Aung San Suu Kyi was finally released. But she was arrested again in 2000, when

30
00:02:56.146 --> 00:03:02.167
she tried to visit another city in Myanmar—something she was not supposed to do. She was

31
00:03:02.168 --> 00:03:09.660
sentenced to another two years of house arrest. She was freed in 2002. Then in 2003 a

32
00:03:09.661 --> 00:03:13.466
mob of government supporters tried to assassinate her. Aung San Suu Kyi was beaten up, and

33
00:03:13.467 --> 00:03:20.696
seventy of her supporters were killed. The police did nothing to stop this. In fact, they placed

34
00:03:20.697 --> 00:03:25.875
her under house arrest again! She wasn't released until November 13, 2010. Since then Aung San Suu

35
00:03:25.876 --> 00:03:31.962
Kyi has continued to speak out against the military government. She and her colleagues have also

36
00:03:31.963 --> 00:03:35.974
been doing humanitarian work to help the people of Myanmar, digging wells and opening health clinics and

37
00:03:35.975 --> 00:03:41.456
schools. OLIVIA: So all together, Aung San Suu Kyi has spent 15 years as a prisoner?

38
00:03:41.457 --> 00:03:49.686
BEN: That's right, Olivia. And journalists who interview Aung San Suu Kyi usually assume that those years

39
00:03:49.687 --> 00:03:55.875
must have been awful. Surprisingly, though, she always says that her time as a prisoner wasn't so

40
00:03:55.876 --> 00:04:03.700
terrible. For one thing, Aung San Suu Kyi was choosing to stay under house arrest. She

41
00:04:03.701 --> 00:04:08.858
could have left anytime she wanted, as long as she left Myanmar and gave up her fight

42
00:04:08.859 --> 00:04:13.753
for democracy. But she insisted on staying in Myanmar until everyone there is free. She's also

43
00:04:13.754 --> 00:04:20.005
said that her suffering was minimal compared to what was endured by the 2,100 political prisoners who

44
00:04:20.006 --> 00:04:24.414
were held in the country's horrible prisons. The worst thing about being under house arrest was that

45
00:04:24.415 --> 00:04:34.073
Aung San Suu Kyi couldn't see her family in England. She has two sons, whom she

46
00:04:34.074 --> 00:04:39.922
didn't see for ten years. When her husband was dying of cancer in 1999, Myanmar's rulers said

47
00:04:39.923 --> 00:04:44.158
she could travel to England to be with him. But Aung San Suu Kyi knew that

48
00:04:44.159 --> 00:04:51.316
she would never be allowed back into Myanmar. So she made the tough decision to stay because—in

49
00:04:51.317 --> 00:04:56.241
her own words—"freedom and democracy are goals which you never give up." During her house arrest, Aung

50
00:04:56.242 --> 00:05:04.210
San Suu Kyi kept herself busy by listening to the radio for most of the day,

51
00:05:04.211 --> 00:05:13.521
trying to stay informed about world events. She especially wanted to hear how the news in other

52
00:05:13.522 --> 00:05:18.385
countries portrayed Myanmar. When she wasn't listening to the radio, she read, practiced French and Japanese,

53
00:05:18.386 --> 00:05:25.312
meditated, and played the piano. Aung San Suu Kyi claims that she's not angry with the people

54
00:05:25.313 --> 00:05:29.610
who imprisoned her. She also says her commitment to the struggle for democracy has never wavered—it's never

55
00:05:29.611 --> 00:05:38.039
occurred to her to take a break from this difficult work. Aung San Suu Kyi remains

56
00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:45.357
optimistic that freedom and democracy will eventually come to her country. Back to you, Olivia. OLIVIA: Thanks,

57
00:05:45.358 --> 00:05:51.323
Ben. Aung San Suu Kyi sure has an incredible and inspiring story. Well, Little Fox readers,

58
00:05:51.324 --> 00:06:01.082
I hope you'll join us again next week for another episode of People in the News !

